Below are the columns that matter for the requested KPI and why they are useful.  
(The names are written exactly as they appear in the tables.)

• `date` : to restrict the analysis to 20160801‑20170430 (the field is `STRING` in YYYYMMDD).  
• `fullVisitorId` : unique user identifier – lets us relate the first session with every later one.  
• `visitStartTime` : session‐time (epoch) – helps to order the visits of the same user.  
• `visitNumber` : sequence of the visit for the user – the “initial visit” is `visitNumber = 1`.  
• `totals.newVisits` : equals 1 in a user’s first recorded session – allows us to isolate “new users”.  
• `totals.timeOnSite` : total seconds on site during each session – “> 300” means more than 5 min.  
• `totals.transactions` (or `totals.transactionRevenue`) : non‑null (or >0) indicates a purchase in the session.  
• `totals.totalTransactionRevenue` : revenue in micros; another way to verify a buying visit.  

Typical conditions that will be used  
1. `date BETWEEN '20160801' AND '20170430'`  
2. `totals.newVisits = 1`     → new users  
3. `visitNumber = 1`          → initial visit (alternative to condition 2)  
4. `totals.timeOnSite > 300` → stayed >5 minutes  
5. A later session with `totals.transactions IS NOT NULL AND totals.transactions > 0` (or `totals.totalTransactionRevenue>0`) and `visitNumber > 1` for the same `fullVisitorId`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------
Ten exploratory BigQuery queries  
(Each query is different, limited to 20 rows, and only uses tables found in the provided list.  
All tables sit in the public project `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample`.)

```sql
-- 1. Description: Look at the available dates in one sample table (ga_sessions_20160808).
SELECT DISTINCT `date`
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20160808`
ORDER BY `date`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Inspect new users on 20160808 and the time they spent on site.
SELECT
  fullVisitorId,
  totals.newVisits,
  totals.timeOnSite
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20160808`
WHERE totals.newVisits = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Find “new‑user, first‑visit” sessions that already last >5 min on 20160808.
SELECT
  fullVisitorId,
  visitNumber,
  totals.timeOnSite
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20160808`
WHERE totals.newVisits = 1
  AND visitNumber = 1
  AND totals.timeOnSite > 300
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Does any of the same users show a later visit on 20160808 with a transaction?
SELECT
  fullVisitorId,
  visitNumber,
  totals.transactions AS tx,
  totals.totalTransactionRevenue AS revenue
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20160808`
WHERE fullVisitorId IN (
        SELECT fullVisitorId
        FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20160808`
        WHERE totals.newVisits = 1
          AND visitNumber = 1
          AND totals.timeOnSite > 300 )
  AND visitNumber > 1
  AND totals.transactions IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Pick another day (20170127) – list new users whose first visit lasted >5 min.
SELECT
  fullVisitorId,
  totals.timeOnSite,
  visitNumber,
  date
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
WHERE totals.newVisits = 1
  AND visitNumber = 1
  AND totals.timeOnSite > 300
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: On 20170127, show the later sessions where those same users purchased.
SELECT
  s.fullVisitorId,
  s.visitNumber,
  s.totals.transactions,
  s.totals.totalTransactionRevenue
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127` AS s
JOIN (
      SELECT DISTINCT fullVisitorId
      FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170127`
      WHERE totals.newVisits = 1
        AND visitNumber = 1
        AND totals.timeOnSite > 300
     ) AS first
ON s.fullVisitorId = first.fullVisitorId
WHERE s.visitNumber > 1
  AND s.totals.transactions IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Combine several daily tables (August 1‑5 2016) using a wildcard to
--     see how many first‑visit sessions lasted over 5 min.
SELECT
  COUNT(1) AS long_first_visits
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_2016080*`
WHERE _TABLE_SUFFIX BETWEEN '1' AND '5'   -- covers 20160801‑20160805
  AND totals.newVisits = 1
  AND visitNumber = 1
  AND totals.timeOnSite > 300;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Quick view of the distribution of time‑on‑site for new users (Apr 15 2017).
SELECT
  APPROX_QUANTILES(totals.timeOnSite, 10) AS deciles
FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_20170415`
WHERE totals.newVisits = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Identify users (Aug 1 2016‑Apr 30 2017) who satisfy both conditions across any day.
--     (Sampling 20 arbitrary users for inspection.)
WITH first_visit AS (
  SELECT
    fullVisitorId,
    MIN(visitStartTime) AS first_ts
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
  WHERE _TABLE_SUFFIX BETWEEN '20160801' AND '20170430'
        AND totals.newVisits = 1
        AND totals.timeOnSite > 300
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
),
buyers AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT fullVisitorId
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
  WHERE _TABLE_SUFFIX BETWEEN '20160801' AND '20170430'
        AND totals.transactions IS NOT NULL
        AND totals.transactions > 0
)
SELECT first_visit.fullVisitorId
FROM first_visit
JOIN buyers USING(fullVisitorId)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Compute the numerator, denominator, and percentage
--       (still limited to 20 rows for demonstration; remove LIMIT for final run).
WITH base AS (
  -- denominator: every new user in the period
  SELECT DISTINCT fullVisitorId
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
  WHERE _TABLE_SUFFIX BETWEEN '20160801' AND '20170430'
        AND totals.newVisits = 1
),
long_first AS (
  -- new users whose first session lasted >5 min
  SELECT
    fullVisitorId,
    MIN(visitStartTime) AS first_ts
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
  WHERE _TABLE_SUFFIX BETWEEN '20160801' AND '20170430'
        AND totals.newVisits = 1
        AND totals.timeOnSite > 300
  GROUP BY fullVisitorId
),
buyers AS (
  -- sessions with a purchase
  SELECT DISTINCT fullVisitorId
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.google_analytics_sample.ga_sessions_*`
  WHERE _TABLE_SUFFIX BETWEEN '20160801' AND '20170430'
        AND totals.transactions IS NOT NULL
        AND totals.transactions > 0
),
final AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT l.fullVisitorId
  FROM long_first  AS l
  JOIN buyers      AS b USING(fullVisitorId)
)
SELECT
  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM final)          AS new_users_long_visit_and_purchase,
  (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM base)           AS total_new_users,
  SAFE_DIVIDE(
      (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM final),
      (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM base)
  ) AS percentage
LIMIT 20;
```

These ten queries progress from simple inspection of single‑day data to a complete (yet still limited) calculation of the desired metric across all relevant days. They illustrate how each column is used and how the conditions come together to arrive at the final percentage.
